---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert image-to-video prompt designer, cinematographer, motion director and visual-continuity specialist.

TASK:
Animate the supplied reference image into a realistic, controlled video while preserving the original image's identity, composition and important visual details.

Treat the supplied image as the PRIMARY VISUAL SOURCE OF TRUTH.

Do not unnecessarily redesign, replace or reinterpret elements that are already established in the reference image.

REFERENCE IMAGE:
[Attach the image to the video-generation model.]

VIDEO GOAL:
[What should happen to the reference image?]

PLATFORM:
[Facebook Reel / Instagram Reel / TikTok / YouTube Short / YouTube / advertisement / website / other.]

DURATION:
[5 sec / 8 sec / 10 sec / other.]

ASPECT RATIO:
[Preserve source / 9:16 / 16:9 / 1:1 / other.]

PRIMARY SUBJECT:
[Person / product / food / object / environment.]

SUBJECT ACTION:
[Describe exactly what the main subject should do.]

SECONDARY MOTION:
[Hair / fabric / steam / liquid / background / environmental movement / none.]

CAMERA:
[Static / slow push-in / pull-back / pan / tilt / tracking / orbit / handheld / other.]

CAMERA SPEED:
[Static / very slow / gentle / moderate / other.]

ENDING FRAME:
[Describe how the clip should end.]

AUDIO:
[None / ambient / dialogue / voice-over / other.]

VISIBLE TEXT:
[Preserve existing text / exact text / no new text.]

ELEMENTS THAT MUST NOT CHANGE:
[List critical identity, product, food, clothing, background or composition details.]

ELEMENTS TO AVOID:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

IMAGE-TO-VIDEO REQUIREMENTS:

1. USE THE REFERENCE IMAGE AS THE FIRST-FRAME AUTHORITY
The generated video should begin as close as possible to the supplied image.

2. PRESERVE THE ORIGINAL SUBJECT
Do not replace the main subject with a similar-looking alternative.

3. PRESERVE CHARACTER IDENTITY
When the image contains a person, maintain:

Face shape
Facial proportions
Skin tone
Eyes
Eyebrows
Nose
Lips
Hair
Age appearance
Distinctive features
Body proportions

4. DO NOT ALLOW FACE DRIFT
The face should not gradually become a different person.

5. DO NOT BEAUTIFY UNNECESSARILY
Avoid:

Face reshaping
Skin whitening
Extreme smoothing
Larger eyes
Different nose
Different lips
Generic influencer appearance

6. PRESERVE HAIRSTYLE
Keep:

Length
Colour
Curl pattern
Parting
General volume

consistent.

7. ALLOW NATURAL HAIR MOTION
Hair may move subtly due to:

Body movement
Wind
Gravity

without changing hairstyle.

8. PRESERVE CLOTHING
Keep the same:

Garment type
Colour
Pattern
Sleeves
Neckline
Fit

unless a clothing change is explicitly requested.

9. PRESERVE ACCESSORIES
Keep:

Jewellery
Glasses
Watches
Headwear
Other distinctive accessories

stable.

10. PRESERVE PRODUCT IDENTITY
For products, maintain:

Shape
Dimensions
Colour
Packaging
Materials
Logo placement
Labels
Distinctive features

11. DO NOT REDESIGN PRODUCTS
Do not add or remove product features unless explicitly requested.

12. PRESERVE FOOD IDENTITY
For food, maintain:

Dish type
Shape
Height
Layers
Texture
Colour
Toppings
Sauce
Serving vessel

13. DO NOT TRANSFORM THE DISH
A single-layer cake must not become a layered cake.

14. PRESERVE OBJECT COUNT
Do not randomly duplicate or remove important objects.

15. PRESERVE PROP IDENTITY
Keep existing:

Plates
Utensils
Furniture
Containers
Tools
Decorations

consistent.

16. PRESERVE THE ENVIRONMENT
The room, kitchen, workplace, landscape or set should remain recognizable.

17. DO NOT REBUILD THE BACKGROUND
Avoid random changes to:

Walls
Doors
Windows
Furniture
Shelves
Counters
Decorations

18. PRESERVE ORIGINAL COLOUR PALETTE
Do not introduce unrelated colours.

19. PRESERVE LIGHTING DIRECTION
Existing highlights and shadows establish the scene lighting.

20. PRESERVE TIME-OF-DAY APPEARANCE
Do not change daylight into night or vice versa unless requested.

21. PRESERVE COMPOSITION
The opening frame should respect the original subject placement.

22. DO NOT RECROP UNNECESSARILY
Keep important elements visible.

23. CONTROL ASPECT-RATIO EXPANSION
If the source image must be extended, continue the environment naturally.

24. DO NOT INVENT IMPORTANT CONTENT OUTSIDE THE FRAME
Outpainting should remain visually plausible and secondary.

25. DEFINE ONE PRIMARY ACTION
The clip should have one dominant movement.

26. KEEP ACTION APPROPRIATE TO DURATION
Do not overload a short clip.

27. START MOTION NATURALLY
Avoid an abrupt first-frame jump.

28. END MOTION NATURALLY
The clip should finish in a visually intentional state.

29. DO NOT TELEPORT OBJECTS
Movement must occur continuously.

30. DO NOT ALLOW OBJECT POP-IN
New objects should not suddenly appear.

31. DO NOT ALLOW OBJECT DISAPPEARANCE
Existing objects should not vanish unless intentionally removed through visible action.

32. MAINTAIN PHYSICAL CONTACT
Hands holding objects should remain correctly attached to them.

33. PRESERVE HAND IDENTITY AND POSITION
Avoid sudden changes in hand size or orientation.

34. REALISTIC HAND ANATOMY
Require:

Five fingers per visible hand when anatomically expected
Natural joints
Correct grip
Natural contact

35. PREVENT EXTRA LIMBS
No duplicated:

Hands
Arms
Legs
Fingers

36. CONTROL BODY MOTION
Movement should remain anatomically plausible.

37. CONTROL FACIAL MOVEMENT
Expressions should transition naturally.

38. AVOID FACIAL FLICKER
Eyes, mouth and facial features should remain stable between frames.

39. NATURAL BLINKING
If appropriate, allow subtle realistic blinking.

40. CONTROL EYE DIRECTION
Maintain gaze or specify a deliberate gaze change.

41. CONTROL MOUTH MOTION
If no speech is requested, avoid unnecessary talking-like mouth movement.

42. DIALOGUE MODE
If speech is requested, keep dialogue short enough for the clip duration.

43. PRESERVE BODY PROPORTIONS
Do not allow stretching or shrinking.

44. USE REALISTIC PHYSICS
Respect:

Gravity
Weight
Momentum
Contact
Friction

45. LIQUID MOTION
Liquids should flow continuously from a logical source to destination.

46. FOOD MOTION
Cutting, lifting, pouring and serving should preserve realistic food structure.

47. FABRIC MOTION
Clothing should react naturally without changing design.

48. STEAM MOTION
Steam should rise naturally and remain subtle.

49. SMOKE MOTION
Use realistic diffusion where relevant.

50. REFLECTION CONTINUITY
Reflections should correspond to moving objects and camera position.

51. SHADOW CONTINUITY
Shadows should remain attached to their objects.

52. CONTACT SHADOWS
Objects should remain visually grounded.

53. PRESERVE PERSPECTIVE
Do not warp object scale during motion.

54. PRESERVE STRAIGHT LINES
Protect:

Architecture
Furniture
Packaging
Screens
Counters

from bending.

55. CAMERA MOTION SHOULD BE SIMPLE
Prefer one dominant movement.

56. STATIC CAMERA MODE
Use when the subject action is already visually interesting.

57. SLOW PUSH-IN MODE
Move gradually toward the subject while maintaining perspective.

58. PULL-BACK MODE
Reveal more environment without changing the scene identity.

59. PAN MODE
Reveal adjacent space smoothly.

60. TILT MODE
Use for controlled vertical reveal.

61. TRACKING MODE
Follow a moving subject while maintaining framing.

62. ORBIT MODE
Use cautiously because it requires generating unseen sides of the subject.

63. AVOID ORBITING WHEN IDENTITY IS CRITICAL
Large viewpoint changes can increase character and product drift.

64. HANDHELD MODE
Keep motion subtle and physically plausible.

65. DO NOT COMBINE CONFLICTING CAMERA MOVES
Avoid requests such as simultaneous orbit, zoom, pan and tracking.

66. CONTROL CAMERA SPEED
Use slow controlled motion for identity-sensitive scenes.

67. AVOID CAMERA JUMPS
The viewpoint should move continuously.

68. MAINTAIN SCREEN DIRECTION
Subjects should not randomly reverse movement.

69. CONTROL FOCUS
Keep the primary subject appropriately sharp.

70. AVOID FOCUS PULSING
Do not allow random blur changes.

71. USE FOCUS PULL ONLY WHEN INTENTIONAL
Specify the start and destination of focus.

72. PRESERVE DEPTH OF FIELD
Keep the source image's general optical character where possible.

73. PRESERVE PHOTOGRAPHIC STYLE
If the source is photorealistic, keep the animation photorealistic.

74. PRESERVE ILLUSTRATION STYLE
If the source is illustrated, maintain the same visual language unless transformation is requested.

75. DO NOT MIX STYLES ACCIDENTALLY
Avoid photorealistic elements appearing inside a consistent illustration or vice versa.

76. PRESERVE IMAGE QUALITY
Maintain:

Texture
Surface detail
Lighting quality
Material realism

77. DO NOT OVERSHARPEN
Avoid artificial edge halos.

78. DO NOT OVER-SMOOTH
Preserve natural skin, fabric, food and material textures.

79. SOCIAL VIDEO MODE
Make the meaningful motion visible quickly.

80. FOOD VIDEO MODE
Prioritize:

Texture
Steam
Gloss
Cutting
Pouring
Serving

while preserving the actual dish.

81. PRODUCT VIDEO MODE
Prioritize:

Product identity
Material
Logo
Surface details
Controlled camera movement

82. CHARACTER VIDEO MODE
Prioritize identity preservation over dramatic motion.

83. BUSINESS VIDEO MODE
Use restrained movement and professional visual language.

84. CINEMATIC MODE
Use cinematic movement without sacrificing source-image fidelity.

85. DO NOT ADD GENERIC AI EFFECTS
Avoid automatic:

Particles
Holograms
Circuits
Neon glows
Magic transformations

86. ADD EFFECTS ONLY WHEN REQUESTED
Effects must serve the intended scene.

87. PRESERVE EXISTING TEXT
Do not alter text already visible in the reference if it must remain readable.

88. DO NOT GENERATE RANDOM TEXT
Avoid new:

Labels
Captions
Signs
Watermarks
Logos

89. PRESERVE LOGOS
If the source contains an approved logo, maintain its identity and placement as closely as possible.

90. DO NOT INVENT BRANDING
No new brand marks.

91. DO NOT INVENT RESULTS
Do not add unsupported:

Statistics
Performance improvements
Transformations
Before/after outcomes

92. DO NOT CREATE FALSE SOCIAL PROOF
No fake:

Ratings
Reviews
Awards
Follower counts

93. DEFINE STABLE ELEMENTS
Explicitly identify what should NOT move.

94. DEFINE MOVING ELEMENTS
Explicitly identify what SHOULD move.

95. USE MICRO-MOTION FOR REALISM
Where appropriate, use subtle:

Breathing
Blinking
Hair movement
Steam
Fabric movement
Environmental movement

96. DO NOT ANIMATE EVERYTHING
Stable areas help preserve image fidelity.

97. CREATE TARGETED NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS
Use only relevant protections such as:

No face morphing
No extra fingers
No duplicate objects
No clothing change
No product redesign
No background warping
No random text
No camera jump
No watermark

98. CREATE FIRST-FRAME FIDELITY CHECK
Verify the opening remains faithful to the reference.

99. CREATE CONTINUITY CHECK
Verify identity, props, lighting, environment and composition remain stable.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- reference image is treated as primary visual truth
- duration is achievable
- aspect ratio is correct
- first frame preserves the source
- primary subject identity remains stable
- face does not drift
- hair remains consistent
- clothing remains consistent
- accessories remain consistent
- product identity remains unchanged
- food identity remains unchanged
- object count remains stable
- hands remain anatomically plausible
- background remains recognizable
- lighting remains consistent
- shadows remain physically coherent
- perspective remains stable
- one primary action dominates
- camera movement is controlled
- no abrupt camera jump occurs
- no random text appears
- no fake logos appear
- no unsupported results are introduced
- final frame looks intentional

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. REFERENCE IMAGE LOCK
List the visual elements that must remain unchanged.

2. VIDEO INTENT
Summarize what should happen during the clip.

3. MOTION PLAN
Separate:

Primary subject motion
Secondary motion
Elements that remain static

4. ACTION TIMELINE
Break the requested duration into simple stages.

Example:
0–2 sec
2–5 sec
5–8 sec

Adapt to the actual duration.

5. CAMERA PLAN
Specify:

Framing
Angle
Movement
Speed
Focus

6. FULL IMAGE-TO-VIDEO PROMPT
Provide one polished, coherent model-ready prompt.

7. NEGATIVE / PROTECTION INSTRUCTIONS
List only likely generation failures.

8. FINAL FRAME
Describe the desired ending state.

9. FIDELITY & CONTINUITY CHECK
Confirm:

Character / product / food identity
Clothing
Props
Background
Lighting
Hands
Camera
Motion

IMPORTANT:
- Treat the supplied image as the primary visual source of truth.
- Preserve identity before adding dramatic movement.
- Keep short clips simple.
- Explicitly distinguish moving elements from stable elements.
- Avoid morphing, duplication, warping, random text and unnecessary redesign.
- Use physically believable motion.
- Preserve the source image's visual style and important details.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Lock the reference image",
    description:
      "Identify the face, clothing, product, food, props, lighting, environment and composition details that the video model must preserve.",
  },
  {
    title: "Choose one main movement",
    description:
      "Decide exactly what should animate instead of asking the model to change the subject, camera, environment and multiple objects simultaneously.",
  },
  {
    title: "Separate motion from stability",
    description:
      "Tell the model which elements should move and which should remain fixed. Stable areas help reduce morphing and scene drift.",
  },
  {
    title: "Define the final frame",
    description:
      "Give the animation a clear destination while keeping the ending visually connected to the supplied reference image.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Reference: Portrait-oriented image of a female food creator presenting a finished cake in a home kitchen.",
    "Platform: Facebook Reel.",
    "Duration: 8 seconds.",
    "Action: She gently presents the cake slightly toward the camera and smiles naturally.",
    "Camera: Very slow push-in.",
    "Secondary motion: Subtle natural breathing, one soft blink and minimal hair movement.",
  ],

  output: [
    "REFERENCE LOCK: Preserve the exact same woman's facial proportions, skin tone, hairstyle, clothing, jewellery, cake appearance, kitchen environment, lighting and original colour palette.",
    "MOTION: She makes one small natural presenting movement with the cake while maintaining a warm expression. Allow only subtle breathing, one natural blink and minimal hair movement.",
    "CAMERA: Use one very slow controlled push-in with no angle change or orbit.",
    "STATIC ELEMENTS: Kitchen layout, countertop, background objects, cake design and lighting remain unchanged.",
    "PROTECTION: No face morphing, no clothing changes, no extra fingers, no duplicated cake, no background rearrangement, no random text and no camera jump.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "Protect identity before adding motion",
    description:
      "Large camera moves, dramatic expressions and complex body actions increase the chance that a reference person or product will drift away from the original image.",
  },
  {
    title: "Tell the model what stays still",
    description:
      "Image-to-video prompts often focus only on movement. Explicitly locking the background, clothing, props and lighting can improve continuity.",
  },
  {
    title: "Use micro-motion when the image is already strong",
    description:
      "A subtle push-in, natural blink, breathing, steam or small hand movement can make a strong still image feel alive without forcing unnecessary scene changes.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Image-to-Video Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create controlled AI image-to-video prompts that preserve character, product and scene identity while adding realistic motion and camera movement."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="VIDEO GENERATION"
    title="Image-to-Video"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Animate an existing image while protecting character identity, products, food, clothing, composition and environment from unwanted AI drift."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Image-to-video",
      "Continuity control",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Character animation",
      "Food videos",
      "Product animation",
      "Reel clips",
      "Creator content",
      "Commercial visuals",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Animate the image without losing what made it work."
    introDescription="Treat the reference as the visual source of truth, lock important details, choose one achievable movement and control the camera so the animation remains connected to the original image."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Add natural motion to a creator image without changing her identity."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Use motion selectively to improve continuity."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Don't have a starting image?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Text-to-Video Prompt when the entire scene needs to be created directly from a written description."
    warningTitle="Reference images can still drift during animation"
    warningText="Image-to-video models may alter faces, hands, products, clothing, backgrounds or text between frames. Review the complete generated clip rather than judging only the first frame."
  />
</BaseLayout>